  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does dominant mean in genetics?",
      "a": "A dominant version of a gene shows its effect even if you have only one copy. In Mendel's peas, one purple version was enough to make the flowers purple.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a dominant allele?",
        "What is a dominant gene?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does recessive mean in genetics?",
      "a": "A recessive version only shows its effect when you have two copies, one from each parent. With one copy it is hidden, but it can still be passed on.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a recessive allele?",
        "What is a recessive gene?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can a trait skip a generation?",
      "a": "Parents can each carry one hidden recessive version without showing it. If a child gets the recessive version from both parents, the trait reappears, even though neither parent showed it.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do traits skip generations?",
        "How can two brown-eyed parents have a blue-eyed child?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What did Mendel discover?",
      "a": "Gregor Mendel, a monk who bred thousands of pea plants, discovered that traits are passed on by units, now called genes, that come in pairs. He found the 3 to 1 pattern of dominant and recessive traits.",
      "asks": [
        "Who was Gregor Mendel?",
        "Why did Mendel use peas?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is eye colour controlled by one gene?",
      "a": "No. Eye colour was once taught as a simple dominant and recessive trait, but it depends on several genes. That is why eye colours vary so much and families can surprise you.",
      "asks": [
        "Is brown eyes dominant?",
        "How is eye colour inherited?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Gregor Mendel published his pea plant experiments in 1866.",
    "When two parents each carry one recessive version, each child has a 1 in 4 chance of getting two recessive copies."
  ],
